(2681057, 364998, 49326, '<td valign="top">Just 5.5. This score should be about 7 or so :). I think it''s very underrated. The lighting is just so awesome! The model is also perfect. Maybe the photographs hasn''t obvious lines.</td>', 'Just 5.5. This score should be about 7 or so :). I think it''s very underrated. The lighting is just so awesome! The model is also perfect. Maybe the photographs hasn''t obvious lines.', '2006-07-31 18:10:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(2681885, 365143, 22301, '<td valign="top">jenny, thanks for your comment on my beach entry shot, I thought I would return the favor. I see two big things I think coud have helped your score. THe first is image size, althought it is 640 px, the acutal size of the file is only 69 kb instead of the whole max allowable 150 kb. This is where the slight pixellation is coming from. Here is a <a href="//dpchallenge.com/tutorial.php?TUTORIAL_ID=26" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">link</a> to the tutorial on preparing photos for submission in to challenges. i notice that lots of photos get marked down soley for this reason on not the quality of the photograph. Second I would tried to have atleast one line either straight or vertical to help let the eye follow some familiar lines. Just some suggestions, thanks again for the comment.<br/>joe</td>', 'jenny, thanks for your comment on my beach entry shot, I thought I would return the favor. I see two big things I think coud have helped your score. THe first is image size, althought it is 640 px, the acutal size of the file is only 69 kb instead of the whole max allowable 150 kb. This is where the slight pixellation is coming from. Here is a link to the tutorial on preparing photos for submission in to challenges. i notice that lots of photos get marked down soley for this reason on not the quality of the photograph. Second I would tried to have atleast one line either straight or vertical to help let the eye follow some familiar lines. Just some suggestions, thanks again for the comment.joe', '2006-07-31 22:35:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(2681905, 365411, 22301, '<td valign="top">Mark, Thanks of your comment on my on the beach entry and thought  I would return the favor. I don''t know whay this photo didnt score higher I think it is great. I love the contrast beteween manmade and natural lines of red rocks. somtimes I just don''t understand the voters. Thankd again for the comment.<br/>joe</td>', 'Mark, Thanks of your comment on my on the beach entry and thought  I would return the favor. I don''t know whay this photo didnt score higher I think it is great. I love the contrast beteween manmade and natural lines of red rocks. somtimes I just don''t understand the voters. Thankd again for the comment.joe', '2006-07-31 22:40:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(2681983, 363821, 22301, '<td valign="top">Margie, thanks for your comment on ym ont he beach entry I tought I would return the favor. The firs thing I notice is the file is only 91 kb and 605 px large, instead of closer to the max 150 kb and 640 px max allowable size. Here is a <br/><a href="//dpchallenge.com/tutorial.php?TUTORIAL_ID=26" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">link</a> to the tutorial for preparing submission for challenges using photoshop. I think this would have helped with all the fine deatils in the photo. I think if you had been a bit better focused it may have helped also. But I love the spider web lines and this a good attempt. thanks again <br/>joe</td>', 'Margie, thanks for your comment on ym ont he beach entry I tought I would return the favor. The firs thing I notice is the file is only 91 kb and 605 px large, instead of closer to the max 150 kb and 640 px max allowable size. Here is a link to the tutorial for preparing submission for challenges using photoshop. I think this would have helped with all the fine deatils in the photo. I think if you had been a bit better focused it may have helped also. But I love the spider web lines and this a good attempt. thanks again joe', '2006-07-31 23:05:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682015, 364888, 46448, '<td valign="top">Very Nice shot! It works great in B/W!</td>', 'Very Nice shot! It works great in B/W!', '2006-07-31 23:17:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682023, 364255, 22301, '<td valign="top">Ingrid, thanks for your commment on my beach entry, thought I would give you one too. I think this is a great example of lines and would have given this a 6 or 7. The only problem I could see would be maybe the composition, looking at where the thirds of the photo falls it is difficult for the eye to have lines to follow. So maybe try a different PoV to help set the shot better, thats really all I can think of, I think this is a great example of lines. Thanks again for your comment.<br/>joe</td>', 'Ingrid, thanks for your commment on my beach entry, thought I would give you one too. I think this is a great example of lines and would have given this a 6 or 7. The only problem I could see would be maybe the composition, looking at where the thirds of the photo falls it is difficult for the eye to have lines to follow. So maybe try a different PoV to help set the shot better, thats really all I can think of, I think this is a great example of lines. Thanks again for your comment.joe', '2006-07-31 23:19:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682137, 364972, 22301, '<td valign="top">Jason, thanks for the comment on my beach entry I thought I would return the comment. This entry is reall cool and I like the way you took every day objects and made an interesting photo out of it. The colors are great and I like how you used different color light sources to do it. Great job, thanks again for the comment and congrats on the high finish in the challenge.<br/>joe</td>', 'Jason, thanks for the comment on my beach entry I thought I would return the comment. This entry is reall cool and I like the way you took every day objects and made an interesting photo out of it. The colors are great and I like how you used different color light sources to do it. Great job, thanks again for the comment and congrats on the high finish in the challenge.joe', '2006-07-31 23:52:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682218, 366197, 22301, '<td valign="top">Heidi, thanks for my comment on my beach entry, I thought I would return the comment. This a good example of lines and really enjoy the color and the contrast between manmade lines in a nature setting. My main suggestion would be to shoot from a really low angle so that the lines are much more emphasized and exagerrated. Other than that I think it is great. thanks again for your comment.<br/>joe</td>', 'Heidi, thanks for my comment on my beach entry, I thought I would return the comment. This a good example of lines and really enjoy the color and the contrast between manmade lines in a nature setting. My main suggestion would be to shoot from a really low angle so that the lines are much more emphasized and exagerrated. Other than that I think it is great. thanks again for your comment.joe', '2006-08-01 00:11:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682226, 365946, 22301, '<td valign="top">A W, thanks for your comment in my beach entry and am giving a comment in return. I tihnk this photo is great, I live the colr and the lines too. The only suggestion I could think of would be to shoot as wide as possible and from as low an angle as possibe to exagerrate the lines even more, just a suggestion, thanks again for the comment.<br/>joe</td>', 'A W, thanks for your comment in my beach entry and am giving a comment in return. I tihnk this photo is great, I live the colr and the lines too. The only suggestion I could think of would be to shoot as wide as possible and from as low an angle as possibe to exagerrate the lines even more, just a suggestion, thanks again for the comment.joe', '2006-08-01 00:14:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682242, 364579, 22301, '<td valign="top">Tim, Welcome to DPC and a great first entry, very creative. I wanted to thank you for your comment on my on the beach entry and return the comment. This is really cool and an awesome finish for your first entry. I can only think of one thing to change, and it really has nothing to do with the photo, just with the size of the photo. Although you havea photo that is 640 px, the file size is only 92 kb instead of closer to the 150 kb max. It may be at 100% with so much black, but it is something to watch out for as it will help keep small details less jaggedy. Really a greay first entry, thanks again.<br/>joe</td>', 'Tim, Welcome to DPC and a great first entry, very creative. I wanted to thank you for your comment on my on the beach entry and return the comment. This is really cool and an awesome finish for your first entry. I can only think of one thing to change, and it really has nothing to do with the photo, just with the size of the photo. Although you havea photo that is 640 px, the file size is only 92 kb instead of closer to the 150 kb max. It may be at 100% with so much black, but it is something to watch out for as it will help keep small details less jaggedy. Really a greay first entry, thanks again.joe', '2006-08-01 00:22:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682387, 363168, 22301, '<td valign="top">Shaun, thanks for your comment on my beach entry I thought I would return the comment to you.  This is a nice macro, but I wish it was larger. I would look at this <a href="//dpchallenge.com/tutorial.php?TUTORIAL_ID=26" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">link</a>, its a tutorial to try and get your photo to the max allowable limits. I notice alot of times photos get marked down for being too small without regard for how well the photo is done.  I really like the colors and exposure of this shot too. Thanks again, joe.</td>', 'Shaun, thanks for your comment on my beach entry I thought I would return the comment to you.  This is a nice macro, but I wish it was larger. I would look at this link, its a tutorial to try and get your photo to the max allowable limits. I notice alot of times photos get marked down for being too small without regard for how well the photo is done.  I really like the colors and exposure of this shot too. Thanks again, joe.', '2006-08-01 01:10:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682394, 364054, 22301, '<td valign="top">Shafaq, Thanks for your comment on my beach entry. This is a really cool take on the lines challenge. I really like the abstractness and light running through the photo. Abstracts can have a hard time scoring well on this site but this is well done. My only suggestion maybe to try a crop that allows the user to see more of the photo, i.e. have a longer vertical, so the photo doesn''t feel so small. but thats just a personal preference. Thanks again for your comment.<br/>joe</td>', 'Shafaq, Thanks for your comment on my beach entry. This is a really cool take on the lines challenge. I really like the abstractness and light running through the photo. Abstracts can have a hard time scoring well on this site but this is well done. My only suggestion maybe to try a crop that allows the user to see more of the photo, i.e. have a longer vertical, so the photo doesn''t feel so small. but thats just a personal preference. Thanks again for your comment.joe', '2006-08-01 01:17:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682530, 364931, 18552, '<td valign="top">Congratulations on your ribbon! This is really such a pretty, colorful picture.</td>', 'Congratulations on your ribbon! This is really such a pretty, colorful picture.', '2006-08-01 02:03:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682573, 365923, 22301, '<td valign="top">Bruce, thanks for your comment on my beach entry I thoght I would give you a comment back. I think I think you found some interesting lines but I think perhaps trying to look at this with another angle to exaggerate the lines may have helped abit. For example shooting closer to the B in bel air and then looking down the words to read the the sign and having the lines exagerrated as they are closer.<br/>joe</td>', 'Bruce, thanks for your comment on my beach entry I thoght I would give you a comment back. I think I think you found some interesting lines but I think perhaps trying to look at this with another angle to exaggerate the lines may have helped abit. For example shooting closer to the B in bel air and then looking down the words to read the the sign and having the lines exagerrated as they are closer.joe', '2006-08-01 02:26:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682688, 364931, 39964, '<td valign="top">Hello:<br/>This is an excelent photo, god eye and fine capture. Colors are supreme, the wheels moving are fantastic. Is a photo that deserves a large print. The crane is interesting, I feel that brakes the colors and the horizont... it makes some kind of "union" betwen the two wheels<br/>My only concern is the inclination in the sooting house -my guess is that is is because the gran angular efect-<br/>Congrats!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-08-01 02:18:16.</i></td>', 'Hello:This is an excelent photo, god eye and fine capture. Colors are supreme, the wheels moving are fantastic. Is a photo that deserves a large print. The crane is interesting, I feel that brakes the colors and the horizont... it makes some kind of "union" betwen the two wheelsMy only concern is the inclination in the sooting house -my guess is that is is because the gran angular efect-Congrats!', '2006-08-01 03:10:31.000000', '2006-08-01 03:18:16.000000', 0),
(2682839, 365396, 19959, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club! <br/> <br/>Hi Brenda,<br/>May I first say welcome to DPC. I see that this is your first competition entry and that you are keen to learn and improve through feedback... well despite the lack of comments to your entry in this challenge I think you will find that you have come to the right place.<br/>The DPC website is a great community of photographers and the challenges will certainly help you to have good reason to keep using your camera...<br/> <br/>I like this picture, it is nicely exposed, warm toned and shows some thought and recognition on fininding a good photo in everyday objects. It is good to develop a photographers ''eye'' and see photos around you.<br/> <br/>Overall the photo works well but the central spot of bright light is slightly over-exposed and distracts a little. I think you should re-visit this subject and try alternative compositions. Perhaps showing the whole lamp as this composition feels slightly cramped.<br/> <br/>Regarding the challenge, I think that you have met the challenge but that your subject choice in this instance is too subtle for the voters. In a challenge (especially a challenge with so many entries) the voters will not spend more than a few seconds on each shot. Subtle shots often don''t score well. Take a look at the results and the comments to the top 20 images to see what voters like...<br/> <br/>I hope that you will enter more challenges as I would like to see more or your pictures. Feel free to drop me an e-mail if you would like further help or advice.<br/> <br/>Kind regards<br/> <br/>Chris</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Hi Brenda,May I first say welcome to DPC. I see that this is your first competition entry and that you are keen to learn and improve through feedback... well despite the lack of comments to your entry in this challenge I think you will find that you have come to the right place.The DPC website is a great community of photographers and the challenges will certainly help you to have good reason to keep using your camera... I like this picture, it is nicely exposed, warm toned and shows some thought and recognition on fininding a good photo in everyday objects. It is good to develop a photographers ''eye'' and see photos around you. Overall the photo works well but the central spot of bright light is slightly over-exposed and distracts a little. I think you should re-visit this subject and try alternative compositions. Perhaps showing the whole lamp as this composition feels slightly cramped. Regarding the challenge, I think that you have met the challenge but that your subject choice in this instance is too subtle for the voters. In a challenge (especially a challenge with so many entries) the voters will not spend more than a few seconds on each shot. Subtle shots often don''t score well. Take a look at the results and the comments to the top 20 images to see what voters like... I hope that you will enter more challenges as I would like to see more or your pictures. Feel free to drop me an e-mail if you would like further help or advice. Kind regards Chris', '2006-08-01 04:57:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682915, 365102, 21816, '<td valign="top"><b>* Greetings from the Critique Club *</b> <br/> <br/>Hi Mike. Ok, let''s be honest here. Why didn''t this photo work with the voters? Pretty much for several reasons. I think what you were going for was a good idea, no problems there. A wooden desk is a surface with lines running through the texture. It was all in the way you executed the photo.<br/> <br/>The main thing, focus focus focus! I''ll be honest and say I''m not too familiar with the camera that you are using. I know there are some users with this camera (SJCarter for instance) who produce some great work. It''s all to do with knowing how to get the most out of your camera. There are two ways you could of gone about shooting this. You could of used the cameras macro mode setting to get in relatively close to the desk to capture it''s detail. A standard P&amp;S camera will operate from about 10 cms from the subject so it would of worked well. Alternatively, you could of pulled back a bit to allow the camera to self focus properly and then cropped it in tight to just show the desk. Either way, you''ve missed one of the main components of a photo here, which is the focus.<br/> <br/>You''ll also notice with your crop of the photo there is a little light spot right at the top in the centre. If you were after just the grain of the desk make sure you crop the image to remove things like this as some people will see it as a distraction. <br/> <br/>Reading through your portfolio I see you''re pretty new to all this. You''ve come to a great place to learn. It''s good to see that you are experimenting with different ideas (looking at your portfolio) which ultimately is the best way to learn. It''s amazing what you''ll pick up by looking through other peoples photos and reading their comments on how they have done it. You mention in your bio that you''re not very experienced with photo editing. Once again, this is a great sight to learn on. Look through the tutorials and try different things and see the results that you get. Keep at it, ask questions, experiment. It''ll all come together. Best of luck with your future entries!<br/> <br/>Neil</td>', '* Greetings from the Critique Club *  Hi Mike. Ok, let''s be honest here. Why didn''t this photo work with the voters? Pretty much for several reasons. I think what you were going for was a good idea, no problems there. A wooden desk is a surface with lines running through the texture. It was all in the way you executed the photo. The main thing, focus focus focus! I''ll be honest and say I''m not too familiar with the camera that you are using. I know there are some users with this camera (SJCarter for instance) who produce some great work. It''s all to do with knowing how to get the most out of your camera. There are two ways you could of gone about shooting this. You could of used the cameras macro mode setting to get in relatively close to the desk to capture it''s detail. A standard P&S camera will operate from about 10 cms from the subject so it would of worked well. Alternatively, you could of pulled back a bit to allow the camera to self focus properly and then cropped it in tight to just show the desk. Either way, you''ve missed one of the main components of a photo here, which is the focus. You''ll also notice with your crop of the photo there is a little light spot right at the top in the centre. If you were after just the grain of the desk make sure you crop the image to remove things like this as some people will see it as a distraction.  Reading through your portfolio I see you''re pretty new to all this. You''ve come to a great place to learn. It''s good to see that you are experimenting with different ideas (looking at your portfolio) which ultimately is the best way to learn. It''s amazing what you''ll pick up by looking through other peoples photos and reading their comments on how they have done it. You mention in your bio that you''re not very experienced with photo editing. Once again, this is a great sight to learn on. Look through the tutorials and try different things and see the results that you get. Keep at it, ask questions, experiment. It''ll all come together. Best of luck with your future entries! Neil', '2006-08-01 06:26:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(2683066, 364408, 46314, '<td valign="top">It''s a pity there weren''t more people in that bottom row. Would have really enhance the whole zig-zag effect. And the bush in the bottom right is a bit distracting.<br/>Even so, I like the overall feeling of the photo.</td>', 'It''s a pity there weren''t more people in that bottom row. Would have really enhance the whole zig-zag effect. And the bush in the bottom right is a bit distracting.Even so, I like the overall feeling of the photo.', '2006-08-01 08:51:05.000000', NULL, 0),
